4. [Collections and Components](#collections-and-components)
   - [Meta Collection](#meta-collection)
   - [System Planes Collection](#system-planes-collection)
5. [Bootstrapping and System Update Process](#bootstrapping-and-system-update-process)
6. [Terminal AI Integration](#terminal-ai-integration)
    - [DBUS Inter Process Control](#dbus-inter-process-control)
    - [Resilient Intelligence](#resilient-intelligence)
7. [References](#references)

## Overview

The `Orchestration Architect` framework is meant to provide a structured approach for efficient system generation and management. With an emphasis on minimal third-party dependencies and leveraging native tools, this solution also focuses on security and composability principles influenced by category theory.

## Framework Structure

The framework is organized into several collections and subsystems that collectively provide diverse functionalities. Each major subsystem, such as storage and network, is represented through composable blocks, facilitating efficient coordination among them.

### Constraints

- Minimize third-party dependencies.
- Implement primitives when useful features are identified.
- Influence design through category theory.
- Ensure continuous growth in velocity, security, and compositional integrity.

## Roles

Roles are the central components of the Orchestration Architect framework. They are composed of several key parts, defined by category R, and include:

- **States:** Minimal objects such as `absent`, `present`, and `info` (identity) morphisms.
- **Tasks:** Defined in `tasks/<role_state>.yml` files like `present.yml`.
- **Configuration Variables:** Packaged into a `<role_name>_params` object.

Each role follows a structured precedence when handling configuration variables.

### Precedence of Variables


